{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b", "content": "**A Whirlwind Week in Japan**\n\nLanding in Tokyo felt like stepping into a living postcard—neon signs flickered above bustling streets while ancient shrines whispered from quiet alleys. My first morning began at the Meiji Shrine, where the towering torii gate framed a serene forest path, offering a stark contrast to the city’s electric pulse just beyond its gates.\n\nAfter a quick ramen slurp in Shinjuku, I hopped on the Shinkansen to Kyoto. The bullet train’s smooth glide made the three‑hour journey feel like a breeze. In Kyoto, I wandered the golden corridors of Kinkaku‑ji, watched tea masters whisk matcha in a traditional machiya, and lost myself among the thousands of vermilion torii at Fushimi Inari at sunset—each gate a tunnel of light and shadow.\n\nA day trip to Nara rewarded me with friendly deer bowing for crackers and the awe‑inspiring Todaiji, home to a massive bronze Buddha that seemed to breathe with the forest’s quiet. Evenings were spent in izakayas, sampling yakitori, sipping sake, and chatting with locals whose warmth turned every meal into a mini‑celebration.\n\nThe final leg took me to Hiroshima. The Peace Memorial Park was a sobering reminder of history, yet the city’s vibrant streets and the iconic Itsukushima Shrine on Miyajima Island—its “floating” torii standing proudly against the tide—showed resilience and beauty intertwined.\n\nLeaving Japan, I carried more than souvenirs; I carried the rhythm of taiko drums, the scent of sakura blossoms, and a renewed appreciation for how tradition and modernity can dance together so gracefully. Until next time, Japan—you’ve earned a permanent spot on my travel heart.", "logprobs": [{"token": "We", "logprob": -0.1887713372707367}, {"token": " need", "logprob": -0.0024945589248090982}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.053322046995162964}, {"token": " output", "logprob": -2.11561679840